## Current status — 2026-09-24

**Implementation is merged in both product layers.**

- Public product PR #474 is merged on `Nostos-Rebirth/main` as `1b0618093cb60851cb0ec54bf2bfa4b882b99e08`.
- Private Cloud PR `Christian-Gennari/Nostos-Cloud#12` is merged.
- Private Cloud currently pins the exact final public merge SHA above.
- Child phases #469–#473 are complete and closed.
- Public and private CI are green on the merged revisions.

The only remaining acceptance gate for this parent epic is **provider-backed live alpha/operator verification against `app.nostos.page`** (real Cloud ingestion → retrieval → grounded answer → source navigation, plus basic tenant/isolation sanity). No further implementation phase is currently tracked here.

---

## Goal

Make **Ask Nostos answer from the user's actual imported books**, rather than relying primarily on model general knowledge.

When a PDF or EPUB is added to Nostos, build a private, regeneratable derived-text representation in the background, index it for fast retrieval, and preserve robust locators back to the exact source file so Ask Nostos can cite/open the passages it used.

This is intentionally more than “export a .txt file”. The derived layer must retain provenance and be safe across different editions, translations, scans, EPUB structures, extractor versions, Cloud/SelfHosted modes, re-imports, deletion, and future migrations.

---

## Why

A useful pattern already proven in practice is:

1. extract a fast searchable text representation once;
2. search that representation for relevant passages;
3. retrieve nearby context;
4. return to the authoritative original PDF/EPUB for source location / citation / reader navigation.

Doing this at ingestion time avoids repeatedly scanning/parsing the original publication for every Ask Nostos turn.

It also gives Ask Nostos a path toward:

- “Ask this book”
- “Ask these books”
- “Ask this collection”
- “Ask my Dostoevsky books”
- eventually “Ask my library”

while grounding answers in the user's own editions.

---

# Core architecture decisions

## 1. The original publication remains authoritative

The imported PDF/EPUB is the source of truth.

Derived text and indexes are disposable acceleration artifacts:

```
original publication
        ↓
derived structured text
        ↓
search/index chunks
        ↓
Ask Nostos retrieval
```

Never make the derived text the only surviving copy of the publication.

If extraction logic changes, derived data must be safely rebuildable from the original.

---

## 2. Never create a global cross-customer book-text cache

Do **not** build a shared Nostos corpus of customer books.

Derived artifacts, chunks, indexes, locators, and future embeddings must respect the same tenant/ownership boundary as the original imported book.

Even when two customers upload byte-identical books, v1 should keep their derived data inside each customer's own storage/index boundary.

Cross-tenant deduplication is explicitly out of scope.

Reason:

- privacy boundary stays simple and auditable;
- no accidental “customer A populates content usable by customer B” behavior;
- deletion semantics remain understandable;
- avoids edition/translation/title/ISBN cache collisions.

---

## 3. Identity is based on the exact source asset, not title/ISBN

A derived artifact must be associated with the exact uploaded file.

Use a cryptographic source-content hash (e.g. SHA-256 of the imported source bytes) plus an extraction schema/version.

Conceptually:

```
sourceHash + extractorVersion
```

Do not use ISBN, title, author, filename, or metadata as the cache identity.

Two files claiming to be the same edition may have different pagination/content and must not silently share locators.

---

## 4. Store the complete derived artifact in object/file storage

Do **not** put one giant book-text blob into the relational database as the canonical artifact.

Cloud concept:

```
customer storage/
  books/{bookId}/
    original/...
    derived/{sourceHash}/
      extraction-v1.jsonl.gz
```

Exact key naming may follow the existing Nostos object-storage abstraction.

SelfHosted should have the equivalent under the local/private book-data area.

The complete derived representation should be:

- private;
- regeneratable;
- versioned;
- compressed where appropriate;
- addressable from book/source identity;
- deletable with the book.

JSONL (optionally gzip compressed) is preferred over a dumb TXT file because provenance/locator metadata must survive extraction.

Do not hard-code JSONL if the existing architecture has a better typed/streamable derived-artifact format, but retain the same semantic properties.

---

## 5. Store searchable chunks/index state in the database

The database should contain the data necessary for fast retrieval, not merely a pointer to an opaque TXT blob.

Each searchable chunk needs at minimum:

- BookId
- source hash / source revision identity
- extraction schema/version
- deterministic chunk/order identity
- chunk text
- source format
- structural context where available
  - chapter
  - section
  - heading path
- exact source locator
- searchable full-text representation/index state
- enough adjacency information to expand to neighboring chunks

Cloud:
- PostgreSQL full-text search first.

SelfHosted:
- SQLite FTS5 (or the project's existing equivalent) first.

Keep the domain/retrieval interface mode-agnostic so Ask Nostos does not need two separate behaviors.

---

# Source locators are a hard requirement

This is the fragile part of the feature and must be heavily tested.

A retrieved passage is not useful enough if Nostos cannot get the user back to the real source.

## PDF

Preserve stable information sufficient to:

- identify the exact source file revision;
- identify page(s) containing the chunk;
- identify text/order offsets where useful;
- open the reader at the correct source page;
- expand retrieval across page boundaries without losing provenance.

Be careful about the distinction between:
- physical PDF page index;
- printed/page-label numbering inside the document;
- text extraction order.

Do not assume they are interchangeable.

Scanned/image-only PDFs are not required to gain OCR in this issue unless OCR already exists in the import pipeline. They must fail/degrade explicitly rather than pretending they were successfully indexed.

## EPUB

Do **not** model EPUB locations as fake fixed page numbers.

Preserve robust structural/book locators such as:
- spine item / resource href;
- EPUB CFI where the current reader stack can support it reliably;
- DOM/text offsets or another deterministic fallback;
- heading/chapter context.

The implementation must survive:
- reflow;
- different viewport size/font settings;
- chapter boundaries;
- XHTML split across multiple spine items;
- nested markup around text;
- duplicate visible strings;
- punctuation/whitespace normalization.

The preferred locator should integrate with the existing Nostos EPUB reader rather than creating an independent locator system that the reader cannot consume.

If current EPUB reader APIs cannot support reliable CFIs end-to-end, document and test the chosen fallback rather than fabricating “page” precision.

---

# Ingestion lifecycle

On PDF/EPUB import:

1. persist the original publication normally;
2. compute/obtain exact source hash;
3. enqueue/schedule derived-text ingestion;
4. extract structured text + locators;
5. write private derived artifact;
6. create/replace searchable chunks/index;
7. mark ingestion state:
   - pending
   - processing
   - ready
   - failed / unsupported
8. make failures retryable;
9. never block basic library import permanently because indexing failed.

Ingestion must be **idempotent**.

Re-running ingestion for the same sourceHash + extractorVersion must not produce duplicate chunks.

If the extractor version changes, Nostos must be able to rebuild safely and switch to the new index without leaving mixed versions active.

---

# Deletion / replacement / portability behavior

## Delete book

Deleting a book must remove or schedule removal of:

- derived artifact(s);
- searchable chunks;
- FTS/index state;
- future embeddings if later added;
- associated ingestion metadata.

No orphaned searchable customer book text.

## Replace/update source

If an imported source file changes:

- compute a new source hash;
- do not reuse old locators/indexes;
- ingest the new revision;
- ensure Ask Nostos does not mix passages between source revisions.

## SelfHosted ↔ Cloud portability

Derived artifacts/indexes are regeneratable and should not become a hard portability dependency.

Prefer portable archives to carry the original user-owned publication/data and allow indexes to rebuild after import unless there is a compelling existing archive contract that makes safe derived-data transport preferable.

Document the decision.

---

# Ask Nostos integration

Add a retrieval capability/tool/service that lets Ask Nostos query imported book text under an explicit user/book/library scope.

Minimum v1 retrieval loop:

```
user question
  ↓
resolve permitted book/library scope
  ↓
lexical/full-text retrieval
  ↓
rank top candidate chunks
  ↓
expand neighboring chunks as needed
  ↓
provide retrieved text + provenance to AssistantOrchestrator
  ↓
answer grounded in retrieved passages
  ↓
return source references that the UI can open
```

Requirements:

- retrieval may only see books the current authenticated/trusted tenant/user may access;
- Ask Nostos must know which retrieved passage came from which book/source;
- the model should be instructed to distinguish retrieved evidence from its own background knowledge;
- source references must survive through the tool/result model to the UI;
- retrieval limits must fit the existing Ask Nostos per-turn tool/token budgets;
- retrieval should not dump entire books into model context;
- neighboring-context expansion must be bounded.

Start with lexical/full-text retrieval.

Do **not** make embeddings/vector infrastructure a requirement for this first implementation.

Design the retrieval abstraction so semantic/hybrid retrieval can be added later.

---

# Grounding behavior

For questions scoped to a book/library where indexed source material exists, Ask Nostos should preferentially use retrieved source content.

It should be possible for an answer to state that the imported text did not contain enough evidence rather than hallucinating a citation from model knowledge.

Do not imply that an answer is grounded merely because the model knows the book.

Every displayed source/citation must correspond to an actual retrieved source locator belonging to the current user's imported asset.

---

# Background processing / UX

The user should not need to manually “generate text”.

Ingestion should happen automatically after supported book import.

Provide enough state for UI to communicate, now or later:

- Indexing…
- Ready for Ask Nostos
- Could not extract text
- Retry

Do not make the entire library UI noisy around this. The initial implementation can expose state minimally if a full UX pass is not warranted.

Ask Nostos must degrade gracefully while indexing is pending:
- do not claim the book was searched if it was not;
- either search other ready sources or explain that this source is still being indexed.

---

# Formats in scope

## v1 required

- text-based PDF
- EPUB

## explicitly not required in this issue

### Full audiobook transcription

Do not automatically transcribe every imported audiobook in this issue.

The compute/cost/storage profile is materially different.

However, locator/data contracts should not preclude a future audio source locator such as:

```json
{
  "type": "audio",
  "startMs": 8071000,
  "endMs": 8164000
}
```

Future options may include:
- supplied/embedded transcript;
- on-demand transcription around a timestamp;
- optional full transcription;
- local SelfHosted transcription.

But none are blockers for this issue.

### OCR of image-only/scanned PDFs

Out of scope unless current Nostos import infrastructure already exposes a reliable OCR path that can be reused without materially expanding scope.

Detect/communicate unsupported/no-text cases correctly.

### Embeddings / vector database

Not required for v1.

This issue should leave a clean extension point for later hybrid lexical + semantic retrieval.

---

# Extraction/chunking requirements

Do not blindly chunk every N characters and discard structure.

Chunking should attempt to preserve useful reading structure while remaining predictable.

Investigate and document:
- paragraph boundaries;
- headings;
- chapter boundaries;
- PDF page boundaries;
- EPUB spine boundaries;
- target chunk size;
- bounded overlap;
- handling very short/very long paragraphs;
- whitespace normalization;
- hyphenated PDF line-break repair where reliable;
- headers/footers/repeated page furniture where feasible without dangerous heuristics.

Each chunk must remain traceable to one or more exact source locations.

If a chunk spans locations/pages, represent that explicitly.

---

# Test strategy

This feature is not complete with a few happy-path unit tests.

Build a dedicated fixture corpus using redistributable/generated test documents. Do not commit copyrighted commercial books merely to test extraction.

## PDF fixture matrix

At minimum:

- simple one-column PDF;
- multi-page chapter crossing chunk boundaries;
- PDF with page labels differing from physical index;
- duplicate text on multiple pages;
- headers/footers;
- line-wrapped/hyphenated prose;
- Unicode / curly quotes / em dashes;
- accented/non-English text;
- blank pages;
- PDF with little/no extractable text;
- replacement of source with a changed PDF;
- very small and reasonably large fixtures.

Verify:
- extracted text fidelity;
- deterministic chunk order;
- page provenance;
- open/go-to-source correctness;
- adjacent-context expansion;
- idempotent re-index;
- deletion cleanup.

## EPUB fixture matrix

At minimum:

- EPUB with one chapter per spine item;
- several chapters in one resource;
- chapter split across resources;
- nested inline markup in sentences;
- duplicate quotations in different chapters;
- headings + subheadings;
- footnotes/endnotes;
- internal links;
- Unicode/non-English text;
- whitespace/entity normalization;
- text split across HTML nodes;
- locator round-trip after reader reflow/font/viewport changes;
- duplicate visible strings requiring location disambiguation.

Verify:
- extraction order follows reading/spine order;
- locator resolves to the intended passage;
- chapter/heading metadata is correct;
- reflow does not invalidate logical navigation;
- re-index remains deterministic.

## Security / tenant tests

Must prove:

- customer A cannot retrieve customer B chunks;
- object-store derived keys are tenant scoped;
- retrieval queries enforce trusted tenant/account context;
- deleting A's book does not affect B even if source hashes match;
- identical byte hashes across tenants do not create a cross-tenant retrieval path;
- untrusted caller cannot choose another tenant/book ID to retrieve text.

## Lifecycle tests

Cover:

- import → pending → ready;
- extraction failure;
- retry;
- process restart during ingestion;
- duplicate job execution;
- extractor-version upgrade;
- stale index cleanup;
- source replacement;
- book deletion during processing;
- book deletion after ready;
- SelfHosted behavior;
- Cloud behavior.

## Ask Nostos integration tests

Use deterministic/synthetic book fixtures and verify:

1. exact quote lookup;
2. paraphrased question with lexical evidence available;
3. same quotation appearing in multiple places;
4. question whose answer requires neighboring chunks;
5. question not answered by the imported text;
6. two-book scope;
7. wrong-book scope must not leak another book;
8. citation/book identity returned correctly;
9. source click opens correct PDF page / EPUB location;
10. token/tool budget remains bounded.

Do not make integration tests depend on a live paid LLM where a deterministic fake/test provider can verify retrieval/orchestration behavior.

---

# Observability

Add useful diagnostics without logging customer book text unnecessarily.

Track events/metrics such as:

- ingestion started/completed/failed;
- format;
- extraction duration;
- source size;
- extracted character/chunk count;
- index duration;
- retry count;
- retrieval duration;
- number of candidate/result chunks.

Avoid logging raw passages in normal production logs.

Errors should identify book/job/source IDs safely enough to debug without emitting the publication text.

---

# Performance / resource constraints

Establish reasonable safeguards for:

- maximum accepted source size consistent with existing upload limits;
- streaming extraction where appropriate;
- bounded memory;
- bounded number of search candidates;
- bounded neighboring-context expansion;
- background concurrency;
- cancellation/deletion races.

Do not load an entire large publication into LLM context.

Benchmark representative fixture books so repeated Ask Nostos search is materially faster than reparsing the PDF/EPUB per turn.

Record the measurements/decisions in the PR or a short design note.

---

# Suggested implementation shape

Use existing abstractions where available rather than forcing these exact names, but the architecture will likely need equivalents of:

- `IBookTextExtractor`
  - PDF implementation
  - EPUB implementation
- `BookDerivedArtifact` / ingestion manifest
- `BookTextChunk`
- typed `SourceLocator`
  - PDF locator
  - EPUB locator
- `IBookTextIndex` / retrieval abstraction
  - PostgreSQL FTS
  - SQLite FTS5
- background ingestion job/service
- Ask Nostos retrieval tool/service
- reader source-navigation adapter

Keep extraction, indexing, retrieval, orchestration, and UI navigation separable enough to test independently.

---

# Implementation sequencing

A single PR may be too large. It is acceptable — and probably preferable — to implement this epic in ordered child issues/PRs.

Suggested sequence:

### Phase 1 — architecture + fixture harness
- audit existing PDF/EPUB reader/import locator capabilities;
- define typed source-locator contract;
- define derived-artifact schema/versioning;
- build generated/redistributable fixture corpus;
- document Cloud/SelfHosted storage/index design.

### Phase 2 — extraction + private derived storage
- PDF extraction;
- EPUB extraction;
- source hashing/versioning;
- background lifecycle;
- artifact persistence;
- robust locator round-trip tests.

### Phase 3 — lexical indexing
- PostgreSQL FTS;
- SQLite FTS5;
- tenant-safe retrieval abstraction;
- lifecycle cleanup/rebuild.

### Phase 4 — Ask Nostos grounding
- retrieval tool/service;
- bounded context expansion;
- provenance in tool results;
- grounded-answer behavior;
- source/citation model.

### Phase 5 — reader navigation + end-to-end hardening
- PDF go-to-source;
- EPUB go-to-source;
- adversarial locator fixtures;
- deletion/retry/version-upgrade tests;
- benchmarks/observability.

If split, this parent issue should remain open until all required phases are verified end-to-end.

---

# Acceptance criteria

- [x] Supported PDF/EPUB imports automatically produce private derived searchable text in the background.
- [x] The complete derived artifact lives in private object/file storage, not as one canonical giant DB blob.
- [x] Searchable chunks/index data live behind the existing per-user/per-tenant database boundary.
- [x] Exact source-file hashing prevents edition/source collisions.
- [x] No global cross-customer book-text corpus/cache exists.
- [x] PDF chunks retain reliable page/source provenance.
- [x] EPUB chunks retain robust reflow-safe source locators compatible with the existing reader.
- [x] Locator round-trip tests cover a broad adversarial fixture set.
- [x] PostgreSQL Cloud retrieval works.
- [x] SQLite SelfHosted retrieval works.
- [x] Ingestion is idempotent, retryable, versioned, and rebuildable.
- [x] Source replacement cannot mix old/new locators.
- [x] Book deletion cleans up derived artifacts and indexes.
- [x] Tenant isolation tests prove book text cannot cross account/customer boundaries.
- [x] Ask Nostos can retrieve relevant passages from a selected book/library scope.
- [x] Ask Nostos receives provenance with every retrieved passage.
- [x] Answers can expose source references that open the user's actual PDF/EPUB at the intended location.
- [x] Ask Nostos does not claim source grounding when retrieval produced no supporting passage.
- [x] Retrieval/context expansion stays inside existing Ask Nostos tool/token budgets.
- [x] Production logs do not routinely contain raw customer book passages.
- [x] Representative benchmark shows indexed retrieval avoids reparsing the original publication per Ask Nostos turn.
- [x] No full automatic audiobook transcription is introduced by this issue.
- [x] No embeddings/vector database are required for v1.
- [x] Documentation/design notes explain how the derived layer can later support semantic/hybrid retrieval and audio transcripts.

- [ ] Provider-backed alpha/operator verification against `app.nostos.page` passes on the merged public/private revisions.

---

# Worker-session instructions

Before implementation:

1. Read `AGENTS.md`.
2. Read this issue in full.
3. Inspect current PDF reader, EPUB reader, import/storage, Cloud object storage, PostgreSQL/SQLite, deployment-mode, authentication/tenant-context, and Ask Nostos orchestration/tooling code.
4. Review recent merged Cloud/Ask Nostos work so this does not bypass existing entitlement, tenant, storage, portability, or per-turn-budget abstractions.
5. Search for existing source-location/page/EPUB navigation models before inventing another one.
6. Use an isolated worktree and branch from **current main**.
7. Do not merge your own PR.

Before writing broad production code, first determine whether this should be one PR or split into child issues according to the sequencing above.

The most important success criterion is not simply “text was extracted.” It is:

> Ask Nostos can cheaply retrieve a passage from the user's exact imported publication and reliably take the user back to the corresponding place in that exact source, without crossing privacy boundaries.

## Цель
Упаковать текущую систему BRODETAIL в продукт, который можно подключить другому детейлингу максимально быстро и без кастомной разработки под каждого клиента.

Рабочее название v0.1: **BRODETAIL Sales OS**.

Это отдельный продуктовый/архитектурный контур. В этой issue НЕ менять текущий production BRODETAIL, `/flyer`, CRM runtime, Agent OS, Task Router, миграции или активные Codex worktree.

## Product promise
Подключаем переписки и заявки детейлинга -> система сама собирает лиды в единую CRM, помогает отвечать, оценивает продажи, показывает потери и постепенно улучшает рекомендации на реальных подтвержденных outcome.

Главная ценность: **владелец не внедряет CRM/AI по частям — он подключает каналы, импортирует услуги/цены и начинает работать.**

## MVP v0.1

### 1. Unified Inbox / Intake
Источники по мере доступности поддерживаемых API/коннекторов:
- VK;
- Avito;
- сайт/формы;
- позже Telegram/WhatsApp/прочие каналы отдельными адаптерами.

Каждое входящее событие -> canonical tenant CRM lead/intake с source attribution и idempotency.

### 2. Lightweight CRM
Минимум:
- лид;
- клиент;
- автомобиль;
- услуга;
- осмотр/запись;
- заказ;
- сумма/оплата;
- outcome/lost reason;
- ответственный менеджер.

Не пытаться заменить полноценную бухгалтерию/ERP в MVP.

### 3. AI Sales Copilot / Agent
Режимы:
- `COPILOT`: предлагает ответ, человек подтверждает;
- `AUTO_ALLOWED`: автоматически отвечает только в разрешённых сценариях;
- `HUMAN_REQUIRED`: цена/технология/риск/нестандартный случай требует человека.

Tenant-specific knowledge:
- услуги;
- цены/ориентиры;
- график;
- материалы/бренды;
- правила записи;
- акции;
- tone of voice.

### 4. Conversation Evaluator
После завершения диалога считать:
- response time;
- был ли qualification question;
- был ли понятный CTA;
- вопрос в конце;
- follow-up;
- предложение звонка/осмотра;
- ошибки/обещания;
- outcome;
- order/revenue where verified.

Выход: score + причины + конкретная рекомендация менеджеру/владельцу.

### 5. Sales Intelligence
Дашборд владельца:
- лиды по источникам;
- response time;
- conversion chat -> booking -> order -> paid;
- средний чек;
- revenue by source/manager/service;
- lost reasons;
- follow-up effectiveness;
- лучшие/худшие диалоги;
- рекомендации, что изменить в скриптах/офферах.

### 6. Reactivation
Только после подтверждения владельцем:
- найти старые диалоги с высоким шансом возврата;
- предложить сегмент и оффер;
- сформировать сообщения;
- SEND по отдельному approval/gate.

Никаких массовых автоматических рассылок по умолчанию.

## Ключевая архитектура SaaS

### Tenant isolation
Каждый детейлинг — отдельный tenant.
Operational data разных студий НЕ смешиваются:
- клиенты;
- телефоны;
- машины/VIN/номера;
- raw chats;
- цены/финансы;
- сотрудники.

### Shared learning layer
Общая «миллионная база диалогов» должна строиться НЕ как общий доступ к raw chats.

Разрешённый общий слой:
- обезличенные паттерны;
- intent/category;
- service type;
- objection type;
- response strategy/features;
- outcome class;
- aggregated conversion evidence;
- embeddings/features после удаления прямых идентификаторов;
- статистически подтвержденные winning/losing signals.

Raw customer conversations остаются tenant-private.

Цель общего слоя: модель знает, например, что при возражении «дорого» для PPF лучше срабатывает X, но не раскрывает чужому детейлингу конкретный чат, клиента, цену конкурента или данные студии.

## Onboarding target
Главная продуктовая метрика внедрения: **от регистрации до первой принятой заявки <= 60 минут**, без разработчика со стороны клиента.

Wizard:
1. Название/город/телефон.
2. Подключить каналы.
3. Импорт/выбор услуг.
4. Ввести цены/диапазоны.
5. График и правила записи.
6. Выбрать режим AI: Copilot/Auto/Human gates.
7. 10-20 тестовых диалогов/regression.
8. GO LIVE.

Допускается assisted onboarding со стороны BRODETAIL на первых клиентах, но продукт не должен зависеть от ручной доработки кода.

## Configuration over customization
Запрещённая стратегия: отдельная ветка/форк приложения под каждый детейлинг.

Все различия клиента должны жить в tenant config/knowledge/rules:
- branding;
- services;
- prices;
- working hours;
- booking policy;
- agent persona;
- allowed channels/actions;
- escalation rules;
- notification targets.

Новый код нужен только для общей функции/нового connector, пригодного нескольким tenant.

## Предлагаемая коммерческая модель для первых продаж
Цены требуют проверки на первых 3-5 пилотах, но стартовая гипотеза:

### START — 14 900 ₽/мес
- unified leads;
- CRM;
- AI copilot;
- basic analytics;
- до оговоренного объёма сообщений.

